<v Maya>Over five times higher, yes.</v>

96
00:10:29.259 --> 00:10:29.659
<v Daniel>Insane.</v>

97
00:10:29.999 --> 00:10:36.406
<v Maya>And for the strategy-enhanced agent... the SEMA, the odds ratio in the financial domain was 7.96. Oh,</v>

98
00:10:36.926 --> 00:10:42.587
<v Daniel>my God. Nearly eight times more likely to choose the inferior option. That is staggering.</v>

99
00:10:42.687 --> 00:10:43.388
<v Maya>It really is. What</v>

100
00:10:43.428 --> 00:10:46.909
<v Daniel>about the emotional domain, though, where people were dealing with the heavy psychosocial issues?</v>

101
00:10:47.189 --> 00:10:52.370
<v Maya>So in the emotional domain, the plain manipulative agent had an odds ratio of 5.52.</v>

102
00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:53.070
<v Daniel>OK.</v>

103
00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:59.792
<v Maya>But here is where we hit the most fascinating and, frankly, the most alarming finding in this entire paper. It is a negative finding.</v>

104
00:11:00.012 --> 00:11:01.552
<v Daniel>A negative finding. What do you mean by that?</v>

105
00:11:01.712 --> 00:11:16.217
<v Maya>Look at the odds ratio for the plain manipulative agent in the emotional domain. Again, 5.52, right? Right. Now look at the SEMA, the super advanced agent armed with your personality profile and a whole toolkit of psychological warfare tactics. Its odds ratio was 5.71. Wait, 5.52 versus 5.71.</v>

106
00:11:16.237 --> 00:11:18.198
<v Daniel>That's basically the same number.</v>

107
00:11:20.939 --> 00:11:32.325
<v Maya>Exactly. Statistically speaking, there was no clear evidence that the fancy psychological tactics of the SEMA agent actually helped overall compared to the basic manipulative agent when it came to emotional advice. Hold</v>

108
00:11:32.405 --> 00:11:38.088
<v Daniel>on. Stop. Are you telling me that the PhD in psychological warfare didn't even matter?</v>

109
00:11:38.568 --> 00:11:38.728
<v Maya>Nope.</v>

110
00:11:39.168 --> 00:11:46.272
